Sign in and store your snouty configuration

Sign in to your tenant, then save the tenant in the global snouty settings file
and store your credential, optionally under a named profile. Login checks the
credential with the tenant, and saves nothing when the tenant rejects it.
Information not provided via args is asked for at the terminal. Without a
terminal, login checks the credential that other commands use, such as
ANTITHESIS_API_KEY, and stores no credential.

Usually login saves the registry that your tenant reports,
`registry.<tenant>.antithesis.com`, and you do not need --registry. When the
tenant reports none, login asks for the registry at the terminal, or keeps the
one saved for that tenant. Use --registry to push your images to a different
registry, such as `registry.example.com/team`: a host, optionally with a port
and a path. That registry must be registered with your Antithesis tenant or
provided by Antithesis. If you are not sure, contact Antithesis support. Then
sign in to that registry with `docker login` or `podman login`.

NOTE: `snouty login` will offer to reuse your existing configuration values,
including any sourced from a local .snouty.toml file or the file specified by
--settings or via the SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH environment variable. However, snouty
login will save the specified configuration and credentials to the "global"
files in your home directory.

Examples:
  snouty login
  snouty login --tenant "mytenant" --registry "registry.example.com/team"
  snouty login --profile "profile"

Usage: snouty login [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --tenant <TENANT>
          

      --registry <REGISTRY>
          

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
